QB.add(30,[{"n":1,"q":"A 55-year-old woman with a history of type 2 diabetes presents for a routine follow-up. Her serum creatinine and potassium levels are normal, but she has microalbuminuria and an HbA1c of 8%. Her blood pressure and pulse are within normal limits, and she is currently on metformin for diabetes management. What should be the next step in her management?","o":[{"t":"Begin a thiazide diuretic"},{"t":"Start insulin therapy"},{"t":"Stop Metformin and start a different OHA"},{"t":"Start an ACE inhibitor"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Start an ACE inhibitor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Starting ACE inhibitors early in the progression of diabetic nephropathy, particularly with the presence of microalbuminuria, has demonstrated significant benefits in reducing the risk of kidney failure and cardiovascular events ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management of Diabetic nephropathy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Oral Hypoglycemic agents SGLT2 inhibitors lower the risk of cardiovascular events (OHAs) and renal failure; they are also recently approved for"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"HFpEF. For HbA1C < 9.5%, OHAs can be used Metformin is withheld if eGFR is < 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 (Option C) , and drugs safe in renal failure can be used like Glipizide Linagliptin Repaglinide"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Insulin 80% of the calculated dose is started if HbA1C > 9.5%"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management of Diabetic nephropathy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antihypertensives In the presence of microalbuminuria, ACEi and ARBs"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"have shown evidence to slow the progression of nephropathy if started in early stages. In hypertension, for BP > 130/90, ACEi or ARBs are preferred If BP is still not under control, Calcium channel blocker (CCB) can be added Thiazide diuretic - in the presence of oedema (Option A) If eGFR <30 ml/min, then the diuretic used - Metolazone. ACEi/ARB comes with a side effect of hyperkalemia; hence, regular monitoring of potassium is necessary. In the presence of hyperkalemia , ACEi/ARB is replaced by CCB Alpha blocker (Prazosin) - hyperkalemia in ESRD."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"For Hyperkalemia K Binding resins (Bind to potassium in the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"gastrointestinal tract and promote its excretion) Sodium polystyrene sulfonate Patiromer Sodium zirconate"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Renal Transplant If eGFR is < 20 mL/min/1.73 m2 ."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 55 year old patient presented with chronic projectile vomiting and weight loss. What is the expected metabolic abnormality to be seen here?","o":[{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hyponatremia"},{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypernatremia"},{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypercalcemia"},{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic acidosis with hyponatremia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct Options A - Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hyponatremia:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypernatremia: This option is not consistent with the clinical presentation. Chronic vomiting typically leads to hyponatremia due to fluid loss and decreased intake, not hypernatremia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypercalcemia: Hypercalcemia is not typically associated with chronic vomiting and would not be the expected metabolic abnormality in this case."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options D - Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic acidosis with hyponatremia: This option does not match the expected metabolic abnormality. Chronic vomiting typically leads to metabolic alkalosis, not metabolic acidosis."}]},{"n":3,"q":"23 year old person with a history of flank Pain and fatigue and history of red coloured urine. On investigation there were more than 20 RBC/ hpf and microscopy showed dysmorphic RBC. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Minimal change disease"},{"t":"PSGN"},{"t":"UTI"},{"t":"Bladder calculi"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - PSGN:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The symptoms described, including flank pain, fatigue, history of red-colored urine, and the presence of more than 20 red blood cells (RBCs) per high-power field (hpf) with dysmorphic RBCs on microscopy, are indicative of glomerular involvement, which points toward a kidney-related issue. Among the options provided, PSGN is the most likely diagnosis for several reasons:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Minimal Change Disease: This is a form of nephrotic syndrome, characterized by massive proteinuria and minimal changes on light microscopy. It typically presents with proteinuria, edema, and normal to slightly elevated blood pressure. It doesn't usually cause gross hematuria or flank pain."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - UTI (Urinary Tract Infection): While a UTI can cause hematuria, it is less likely to cause dysmorphic RBCs and would not explain the flank pain and fatigue."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Bladder Calculi: Bladder calculi (stones) might cause hematuria, but they would not explain the presence of dysmorphic RBCs, and typically, the pain associated with bladder stones is localized to the lower abdomen and not the flanks."}]},{"n":4,"q":"All the following are features of polycystic disease of kidneys except:","o":[{"t":"Hematuria"},{"t":"Hypertension"},{"t":"Renal failure"},{"t":"Erythrocytosis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Erythrocytosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Erythrocytosis refers to an elevated red blood cell count. While it is not a typical feature of PKD, in some cases, PKD can lead to the development of renal cell carcinoma, which may cause erythrocytosis as a paraneoplastic syndrome. However, this is not a common finding in most cases of PKD."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Erythrocytosis refers to an elevated red blood cell count. While it is not a typical feature of PKD, in some cases, PKD can lead to the development of renal cell carcinoma, which may cause erythrocytosis as a paraneoplastic syndrome. However, this is not a common finding in most cases of PKD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Hematuria : Hematuria, which is the presence of blood in the urine, can occur in PKD due to the rupture or bleeding within the cysts. It is a common symptom of PKD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hypertension : High blood pressure is a common feature of PKD. The cysts in the kidneys can interfere with normal kidney function, leading to increased renin secretion and subsequent hypertension."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Renal Failure : PKD is a progressive condition that can eventually lead to kidney failure. The numerous cysts gradually replace the healthy kidney tissue, impairing kidney function over time."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 30-year-old male is admitted to the medicine ward with complaints of vomiting and diarrhea. Lab investigations reveal a serum potassium level of 3.2 mEq/L. All of the following can be signs of underlying dyselectrolytemia except:","o":[{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Quadriplegia"},{"t":"Diabetes insipidus"},{"t":"Cerebral edema"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Cerebral edema:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given scenario points towards hypokalemia. Cerebral edema is related to fluctuations in the value of sodium (hyponatremia). Potassium does not play a role in plasma osmolality hence do not cause cerebral edema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Ventricular tachycardia: Hypokalemia can contribute to the development of Torsades de Pointes a type of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Quadriplegia: Hypokalemia causes excessive hyperpolarization in skeletal muscles, impairing their ability to contract and leading to weakness and potential paralysis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Diabetes insipidus: Hypokalemic polyuria results from both excessive drinking (central polydipsia) and the kidneys' reduced response to antidiuretic hormone (AVP-resistant renal concentrating defect)."}]},{"n":6,"q":"Which of the following is the most likely condition responsible for a 35-year-old male experiencing nausea, diarrhea, and vomiting, with ABG results indicating pH of 7.55, PCO2 of 50 mmHg, and HCO3- of 44 mmol?","o":[{"t":"Respiratory Acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory Alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic Acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic Alkalosis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Metabolic Alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Given that all three parameters are moving in the same direction, indicating a metabolic disorder, and with an increase in pH, the condition is likely metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B & C are correct."}]},{"n":7,"q":"\"Which of the following best characterizes the acid-base status of a 45-year-old diabetic patient presenting to the emergency department with abdominal pain, tachypnea, and tachycardia, whose arterial blood gas analysis reveals the following: pH: 7.34 Sodium (Na): 135 Potassium (K): 5 Bicarbonate (HCO3): 12 Chloride (Cl): 92 RBS = 450 mg/dl","o":[{"t":"Non-Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (NAGMA)"},{"t":"High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA)"},{"t":"Compensated Metabolic Alkalosis"},{"t":"Respiratory Acidosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pH and bicarbonate levels are reduced hence it indicates metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Formula for Anion gap = [Na + ] - [Cl - + HCO 3 - ]"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, Anion gap = 135 - [92 + 12] = 31"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the anion gap is >12 mEq/L which indicates a High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C & D are incorrect."}]},{"n":8,"q":"Which of the following is not a cause of chronic bipedal edema?","o":[{"t":"Chronic liver disease"},{"t":"Nephrotic syndrome"},{"t":"Chronic venous insufficiency"},{"t":"Lupus nephritis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Chronic venous insufficiency:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic venous insufficiency typically presents with unilateral pedal edema rather than bilateral. The edema, often pitting, can be limited to the ankles, extend upwards to the knees, or even involve the thighs in severe instances. With progression, the edema may transition from being primarily pitting to becoming more indurated over time."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Chronic liver disease: In chronic liver disease (CLD), decreased albumin levels result in reduced oncotic pressure, contributing to the development of bipedal edema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Nephrotic syndrome: Nephrotic syndrome also leads to bipedal edema by lowering oncotic pressure due to decreased albumin levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Lupus nephritis: Lupus nephritis results in bipedal edema due to the presence of hyperaldosteronism."}]},{"n":9,"q":"Which of the following is most likely based on the lab values given? Blood pH: 7.3 (indicating mild acidosis) Sodium (Na+): 134 mEq/L (mildly decreased) Potassium (K+): 2.8 mEq/L (hypokalemia) Chloride (Cl-): 113 mEq/L (elevated) Bicarbonate (HCOnn): 12 mEq/L (low, indicating metabolic acidosis) Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN): 3 mg/dL (low-normal) Creatinine: 0.6 mg/dL (normal) Urinary pH: 9.4 (alkaline)","o":[{"t":"Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA) Type 1 (Distal RTA)"},{"t":"Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA) Type 2 (Proximal RTA)"},{"t":"Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA) Type 4"},{"t":"High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA)"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - RTA Type 1 (Distal RTA) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"RTA Type 1, also known as distal RTA, is characterized by the inability of the distal tubules of the kidneys to excrete hydrogen ions, leading to an alkaline urinary pH (usually >5.5). Patients typically have a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia (low potassium), and a low serum bicarbonate level. In this case, the blood pH is mildly acidotic (7.3), with a low HCOnn (12 mEq/L) and hypokalemia (K+ of 2.8 mEq/L). The urine pH is markedly alkaline (9.4), which is consistent with RTA Type 1."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - RTA Type 2 (Proximal RTA) : RTA Type 2, or proximal RTA, involves a defect in bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule, leading to a loss of bicarbonate in the urine and subsequent metabolic acidosis. Urine is typically acidic (pH < 5.5) after the loss of bicarbonate is maximized. Patients can have hypokalemia, but the urinary pH would not remain as high (9.4) after the plasma bicarbonate is depleted, making this less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - RTA Type 4 : RTA Type 4 is characterized by hyperkalemia (high potassium) and a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis. This type results from hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance. In this case, the patient has hypokalemia (K+ of 2.8 mEq/L), not hyperkalemia, making RTA Type 4 an unlikely diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA) : HAGMA is characterized by a high anion gap due to the accumulation of unmeasured anions (e.g., lactate, ketoacids, toxins). The calculated anion gap here is normal (AG = Na - [Cl + HCOn] = 134 - [113 + 12] = 9), which does not support a diagnosis of HAGMA. Therefore, HAGMA is not the correct answer."}]},{"n":10,"q":"What is the recommended treatment for nephrotic syndrome in children?","o":[{"t":"Steroids"},{"t":"Steroids and cyclophosphamide"},{"t":"Cyclophosphamide"},{"t":"ACE inhibitors"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Steroids are the first-line treatment for nephrotic syndrome , particularly in minimal change disease, which is the most common cause in children . Corticosteroids work by reducing inflammation and suppressing the immune response that leads to proteinuria. The typical initial treatment consists of oral prednisone, with most patients showing a response within 4-8 weeks of therapy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feat Steroid-Responsive Nephrotic Steroid-Resistant Nephrotic ure Syndrome Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Defin Nephrotic syndrome that responds Nephrotic syndrome that does not ition well to corticosteroid treatment. improve despite adequate"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"corticosteroid treatment (e.g., prednisolone at 2 mg/kg/day for 6 weeks). Types: Initial Steroid Resistance: Lack of response from the onset. Late Steroid Resistance: Resistance develops after initial response."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Com Minimal Change Nephrotic It is often associated with Focal mon Syndrome (MCNS) accounts for Segmental Glomerulosclerosis Type about 80% of cases in children. (FSGS) or genetic mutations"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"affecting podocytes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diag Diagnosed without kidney biopsy; Further investigation (e.g., kidney nosis characterised by heavy proteinuria, biopsy) is required to determine the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"low serum albumin, and no underlying cause. significant glomerular damage."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Man Responds to high-dose Treatment may include age corticosteroids (e.g., prednisolone) , immunosuppressive agents, ment leading to remission of proteinuria calcineurin inhibitors, or other"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and oedema within 10-14 days. therapies, as corticosteroids are"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"often ineffective."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Steroids and cyclophosphamide (Option B): While this combination may be used in steroid-resistant cases or specific types of nephrotic syndrome, it is not the first-line treatment for nephrotic syndrome in children, which is mainly due to Minimal change disease. The addition of cyclophosphamide is reserved for cases that fail to respond to steroids alone or have frequent relapses. Also, the potential toxicity of cyclophosphamide makes it unsuitable as an initial therapy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide (Option C): Using cyclophosphamide alone is not the recommended initial treatment for nephrotic syndrome in children. It is typically used as a second-line agent in steroid-resistant cases or specific pathological variants."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ACE inhibitors (Option D): While ACE inhibitors can help reduce proteinuria and are often used as adjunctive therapy, they are not the primary treatment for nephrotic syndrome. They are typically used in conjunction with immunosuppressive therapy, which is the mainstay for treating the underlying cause of nephrotic syndrome."}]},{"n":11,"q":"The patient is having the following hand movements. In which of the following conditions would this be seen?","o":[{"t":"Thyrotoxicosis"},{"t":"Hypercarbia"},{"t":"Hemiballismus"},{"t":"Hypoxia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Hypercarbia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asterixis or flapping tremors is a sign of metabolic encephalopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This can occur due to increased levels of ammonia (hepatic encephalopathy), increased levels of urea, or increased levels of CO 2 ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypercarbic encephalopathy occurs most frequently in patients with a history of chronic CO2 retention who are receiving oxygen therapy for emphysema or chronic pulmonary disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asterixis can be elicited by having patients extend their arms and bend their wrists back. In this maneuver, patients who are encephalopathic have a “liver flap”—that is, a sudden forward movement of the wrist."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A- Thyrotoxicosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyrotoxicosis is ruled out as it presents with fine tremors."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options C- Hemiballismus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemiballismus will have widing flinging movements at the shoulders."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options D- Hypoxia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypoxia causes disorentation, and irritability."}],"qi":[6179]},{"n":12,"q":"A 22-year-old patient has a fever with severe shortness of breath and red currant jelly sputum. On examination, he has altered sensorium with an RR of 40/min. Lab investigations show serum creatinine of 1.9 mg/dl and BUN with 5 mmol/L (Normal:<7 mmol/L). Which of the following is correct about its management?","o":[{"t":"OPD management with oral drugs"},{"t":"IPD management with IV drugs"},{"t":"ICU management with IV drugs"},{"t":"ICU management with oral drugs"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option-B - IPD management with IV drugs:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To decide if the patient needs hospitalization or not CURB-65 score needs to be calculated."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CURB-65 score"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Confusion 1 point"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"BUN ‡ 7 mmol/L 1 point"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Respiratory rate > 30/min 1 point"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"BP<90/60 mm Hg 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the history on the above history and the laboratory parameters, the CURB-score is 2 (1 point each for respiratory rate and altered sensorium) and the patient needs to be admitted to the hospital"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, C, and D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As the CURB-65 score is 2, the patient requires hospitalization."}]},{"n":13,"q":"Which of the following antidiabetic drugs can cause glucosuria?","o":[{"t":"Glimepiride"},{"t":"Exenatide"},{"t":"Acarbose"},{"t":"Empagliflozin"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Empagliflozin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proximal convoluted tubules of the nephron have SGLT-2 transporters that promote the reabsorption of sodium and glucose back into the blood."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"On administration of SGLT-2 inhibitors (Empagliflozin, Canagliflozin, Dapagliflozin) promotes the excretion of sodium and glucose in the urine leading to glucosuria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These drugs are not recommended in patients with eGFR <45 ml/min and they cause UTI."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Effects of SGLT-2 Inhibitors Reduces the progression of CKD Has a diuretic effect, loses sodium SBP: Decreases by 3-6 mmHg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduces the progression of CKD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Has a diuretic effect, loses sodium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SBP: Decreases by 3-6 mmHg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduces the progression of CKD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Has a diuretic effect, loses sodium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SBP: Decreases by 3-6 mmHg"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Glimepiride:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glimepiride causes hypoglycemia, weight loss"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Exenatide:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exenatide causes weight loss and reduces cardiovascular mortality by stabilizing atherosclerotic plaque."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acarbose:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acarbose is used to control the post-prandial glucose spikes and they may cause diarrhea."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A patient having hypertension is on thiazides and is complaining of fatigue and hypokalemia. Which of the following drugs can prevent potassium loss?","o":[{"t":"Furosemide"},{"t":"Amiloride"},{"t":"Acetazolamide"},{"t":"Indapamide"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Amiloride:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low-dose thiazide diuretics are safe, and efficacious and may be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive drugs like beta blockers, ACEIs, or ARBs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Owing to increased side effects (hypokalemia, insulin resistance, increased cholesterol) high doses are not recommended."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amiloride is a potassium sparring diuretic that acts by inhibiting ENac in the distal nephron."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a weak anti-hypertensive but may be used in combination with a thiazide to protect against hypokalemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amiloride is contraindicated in renal failure and hyperkalemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Furosemide:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Furosemide is used in patients with reduced GFR, CHF, sodium retention/edema due to other reasons"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acetazolamide:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acetazolamide is used in the treatment of glaucoma, altitude sickness, CHF, and periodic paralysis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Indapamide:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indapamide is a diuretic agent used in the treatment of hypertension."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A patient with a history of hypertension and taking multiple anti-hypertensive medications presents at the outpatient department (OPD). The provided ECG results indicate a particular drug as the cause for the observed ECG findings. Which of the following medications is accountable for these ECG findings?","o":[{"t":"Prazosin"},{"t":"Metoprolol"},{"t":"Hydrochlorothiazide"},{"t":"Spironolactone"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Spironolactone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG of the patient shows signs of hyperkalemia that include peaked T-waves, PR interval widening, and QRS interval widening."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperkalemia is a common side effect of potassium-sparing diuretics like spironolactone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spironolactone causes the prevention of excessive K + excretion in the urine and reduced water retention causing increased levels of potassium."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other drugs causing hyperkalemia Amiloride Triamterene ACE inhibitors ARBs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amiloride"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triamterene"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ACE inhibitors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ARBs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amiloride"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triamterene"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ACE inhibitors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ARBs"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Prazosin: Prazosin is an alpha-channel blocker that has a common side of orthostatic hypotension."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Metoprolol: Metoprolol is a beta blocker that causes bradycardia, hypotension, and arrhythmias when used for a long period of time."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hydrochlorothiazide: Hydrochlorothiazide side effects include hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, hypercalcemia, and hypokalemia."}],"qi":[6186]},{"n":16,"q":"A patient with chronic kidney disease came with complaints of vomiting and diarrhea. His blood gas reports show pH = 7.40, HC03- = 23 mEq/L, Na+ = 145 mEq/L, anion gap= 100 mEq/L. What is your probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"No acid base abnormality"},{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"},{"t":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - High anion gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal pH of 7.40- Indicates no primary acid-base abnormality. However, the"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bicarbonate (HCO 3 -) level of 23 mEq/L falls within the normal range- Suggests that there is a compensatory mechanism in place."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium (Na + ) level of 145 mEq/L is also within the normal range."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated anion gap of 100 mEq/L, which indicates the presence of an underlying metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis is commonly seen in conditions such as diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, and renal failure. In this case, the patient's chronic kidney disease is likely contributing to the development of high anion gap metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased anion gap but HCO 3 is not reciprocally reduced indicating presence of metabolic alkalosis component."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above findings suggests a diagnosis of High anion gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - No acid-base abnormality: This choice is incorrect because the blood gas reports indicate the presence of an abnormality, specifically a high anion gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Respiratory acidosis: The blood gas reports do not show any abnormalities in the partial pressure of carbon dioxide (pCO 2 ), which is a key indicator of respiratory acidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Metabolic alkalosis: This choice is incorrect because it only accounts for the metabolic alkalosis component of the patient's acid-base disturbance."}]},{"n":17,"q":"High anion gap acidosis with low bicarbonate levels is seen in all of the following conditions except:","o":[{"t":"Renal disorders"},{"t":"Diabetic ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Lactic acidosis"},{"t":"Salicylate poisoning"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Renal disorders:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High anion gap acidosis with low bicarbonate levels is a characteristic finding in several metabolic acidosis conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, renal disorders, specifically renal tubular acidosis (RTA), typically present with a normal anion gap acidosis rather than a high anion gap acidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Diabetic ketoacidosis: Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a common cause of high anion gap metabolic acidosis. In DKA, there is an increase in the production of ketones (such as beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate), leading to the acidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Lactic acidosis: Lactic acidosis occurs when there is an excessive production or reduced clearance of lactic acid. Lactic acidosis is typically associated with a high anion gap and low bicarbonate levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Salicylate poisoning: Salicylate poisoning, often seen with aspirin overdose, can lead to high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Bicarbonate levels are typically decreased in salicylate poisoning."}]},{"n":18,"q":"In methanol intoxication, which of the subsequent electrolyte abnormalities is observed?","o":[{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis with high anion gap"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis with high anion gap"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis with normal anion gap"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis with normal anion gap"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Metabolic acidosis with high anion gap:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Methanol intoxication leads to the formation of toxic metabolites, including formic acid, which causes metabolic acidosis. The acidosis results from the accumulation of formic acid and its metabolites, leading to an increased anion gap."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other causes Ketoacidosis Uremia Lactic acidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ketoacidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Uremia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactic acidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ketoacidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Uremia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactic acidosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, C & D are incorrect."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 5 year old child presents to the OPD with pedal edema and facial puffiness for the past 5 days. BP was 126/80 mm Hg, proteinuria 3+. Urine examination shows 100 RBCs/hpf. What is the most likely diagnosis ?","o":[{"t":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"Minimal change disease"},{"t":"Membranous glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is type 3 hypersensitivity reaction followed by group A beta-hemolytic streptococci."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It commonly affects children and presents with symptoms such as pedal edema (swelling of the feet), facial puffiness, hypertension (elevated blood pressure), and proteinuria (presence of excess protein in the urine)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In PSGN, the immune response triggered by the streptococcal infection causes formation of immune complexes by an antigen called Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B (SPEP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is distributed in the renal parenchyma damagin the blood vessels contributing to liguria, increaased renin (HTN)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Minimal change disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hematuria (presence of RBCs in urine) is less common in minimal change disease, and when present, it's usually microscopic rather than gross."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents with nephrotic syndrome characterized by proteinuria (typically massive), hypoalbuminemia, and edema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Membranous glomerulonephritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents with nephrotic syndrome but is more common in adults."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proteinuria is usually moderate to severe and may not be accompanied by gross hematuria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Edema and hypertension can be present but are less common in children."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents with nephrotic syndrome but is also more common in adults."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proteinuria can be massive but is typically accompanied by microscopic hematuria rather than gross hematuria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Edema and hypertension may be present but are less common in children."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 56-year-old man presents to the emergency with severe shortness of breath and fatigue for the past 3 hours. He reports vomiting and epigastric pain 2 days back, for which he took an anti-emetic. His past medical history is significant for hypertension and diabetes mellitus type 1. The lab values show BSL=356mg/dL. The doctor advises immediate ABG analysis, which reveals a pH=7.32, pCO2=38mEq/L, and HCO3-=15mEq/L. This type of acidosis has increased the anion gap. Which of the following condition does not belong to the same classification of metabolic acidosis as this patient?","o":[{"t":"Renal failure"},{"t":"Lactic acidosis"},{"t":"Renal aciduria"},{"t":"Aspirin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Renal aciduria:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal aciduria refers to a condition characterized by the inability of the kidneys to effectively reabsorb bicarbonate or excrete hydrogen ions, leading to a decreased bicarbonate concentration and metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, renal aciduria presents with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a form of renal tubular acidosis (RTA)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conditions with High anion gap metabolic acidosis Ketoacidosis Uremia Lactic acidosis Toxins"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ketoacidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Uremia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactic acidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Toxins"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ketoacidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Uremia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactic acidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Toxins"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A - Renal failure: Renal failure can be classified as high anion gap metabolic acidosis or normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, depending on the underlying cause. Therefore, renal failure can be associated with metabolic acidosis with an increased anion gap, similar to the patient's condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options B - Lactic acidosis: Lactic acidosis occurs when there is an overproduction or impaired clearance of lactic acid. Lactic acidosis is typically associated with an increased anion gap metabolic acidosis, similar to the patient's presentation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options D - Aspirin: Aspirin toxicity is characterized by increased production of organic acids and impaired respiratory compensation. This type of metabolic acidosis is also associated with an increased anion gap, similar to the patient's condition."}]},{"n":21,"q":"Calculate the anion gap from the following values: Na+ = 137 mmol/L K+ = 4 mmol/L CI- = 100 mmol/L HCO3- = 15 mmol/L","o":[{"t":"26 mmol/L"},{"t":"16 mmol/L"},{"t":"10 mmol/L"},{"t":"12 mmol/L"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - 22 mmol/L:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anion Gap = [Na + +K + ] - ([Cl - ] + [HCO 3 -])"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Given the values: [Na + ] = 137 mmol/L"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"K + = 4 mmol/L"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"[Cl - ] = 100 mmol/L"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"[HCO 3 -] = 15 mmol/L"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anion Gap = (137 + 4) - (100 + 15) = 141 - 115 = 26 mmol/L"}]},{"n":22,"q":"Which of the following are not included in the components of the MELD (2016) score?","o":[{"t":"Bilirubin"},{"t":"INR"},{"t":"Albumin"},{"t":"Creatinine"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Albumin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Albumin is not a parameter is used in calculating MELD score."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The MELD (Model for End-Stage Liver Disease) score is used to determine the mortality of a liver disease patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parameters Serum bilirubin Serum creatinine INR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum creatinine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"INR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The lower limit is 6, and the upper limit is 40."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The higher the score, higher the chances of enlistment for an orthoptic liver transplant."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"United network for organ sharing > 17, enlist for orthoptic liver tranplant"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum creatinine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"INR"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, B, & D - Bilirubin, INR & Creatinine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"All these are parameters of MELD score."}]},{"n":23,"q":"Rinku devi, a 26 years old female, labourer by occupation presented to you with complains of pain abdomen, nausea and restlessness since 4 hours. She gives h/o 2 episodes of hematuria. USG showed a 3×3 cm stone in Right renal pelvis. There is no evidence of hydronephrosis. Which of following is the best procedure for management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"ESWL"},{"t":"PCNL"},{"t":"Antegrade Pyeloplasty"},{"t":"Retrograde pyeloplasty"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - PCNL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PCNL (Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy) is the best procedure for the management of kidney stones > 2 cm in size."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Management of Kidney stones Ketrolac/Tramadol Euvolemia is achieved via IVF (prevents dehydration) Alpha-1 blocker (Tamsulosin) can be given to relax ureter and expels the stone. <2 cm kidney stone- Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (contraindicated in pregnancy bleeding diathesis) > 2 cm kidney stone- PCNL (Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy) Ureteric stone (lower 1/3rd): Endourological appraoch- Dormian basket."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ketrolac/Tramadol"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Euvolemia is achieved via IVF (prevents dehydration)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alpha-1 blocker (Tamsulosin) can be given to relax ureter and expels the stone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"<2 cm kidney stone- Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (contraindicated in pregnancy bleeding diathesis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"> 2 cm kidney stone- PCNL (Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ureteric stone (lower 1/3rd): Endourological appraoch- Dormian basket."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ketrolac/Tramadol"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Euvolemia is achieved via IVF (prevents dehydration)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alpha-1 blocker (Tamsulosin) can be given to relax ureter and expels the stone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"<2 cm kidney stone- Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (contraindicated in pregnancy bleeding diathesis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"> 2 cm kidney stone- PCNL (Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ureteric stone (lower 1/3rd): Endourological appraoch- Dormian basket."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - ESWL: ESWL (Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy) is a non-invasive procedure done for kidney stones <2 cm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Antegrade Pyeloplasty: Antegrade Pyeloplasty is a surgical procedure used to treat ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Retrograde pyeloplasty: Retrograde Pyeloplasty is also a surgical procedure used for the treatment of UPJ obstruction."}]},{"n":24,"q":"Identify the urinary crystal shown below:","o":[{"t":"Calcium oxalate"},{"t":"Calcium phosphate"},{"t":"Uric acid"},{"t":"Cysteine"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Calcium oxalate:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above given image is the crystal of calcium oxalate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It the most common type of renal stone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is also the leading cause of recurrent kidney stone formation- Idiopathic hypercalcinuria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"On microscopic examination, the crystal is envelope shaped."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcium oxalate can be dihydrate or monohydrate forms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mono hydrate form is dumbell shaped or has a picket fence appearance in the urine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Calcium phosphate: Calcium phosphate crystals on microscopic examination are Rosette shaped."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Uric acid: Uric acid crystals have no particular shape on microscopic examination."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Cysteine: Cysteine crystal are Hexagonal shaped on microscopic examination."}],"qi":[6191]},{"n":25,"q":"A male patient who is on diuretics presents with muscle weakness An ECG was done which showed flat T waves and prominent U waves. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Hypokalaemia"},{"t":"Hyperkalaemia"},{"t":"Hypomagnesemia"},{"t":"Hypernatremia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypokalaemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most likely diagnosis is hypokalemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diuretics such as thiazides and loop diuretics can cause potassium loss , which can influence hypokalemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG judgments of flat T waves and prominent U waves are characteristic of hypokalemia . Other ECG changes include ST segment depression, pseudo P pulmonale, increased PR and QU prolongation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other causes of hypokalemia are alkalosis, trauma, thyrotoxic periodic paralysis, vomitting, diarrhea, Bartter syndrome, Conn syndrome, Gitelman syndrome, Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other manifestations of hypokalemia can involve muscle weakness, fatigue, muscle spasm, and palpitations."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hyperkalemia: ECG changes associated with hyperkalemia usually involve Tall tented T waves, ST elevation is coved pattern, reduced amplitude of P wave, PR prolongation, Broad QRS complex and sine wave pattern."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypomagnesemia : ECG changes associated with hypomagnesemia usually contain prolongation of the QT interval."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypernatremia : ECG changes associated with hypernatremia are unique but can involve a shortened QT interval and ST segment changes ."}]},{"n":26,"q":"What is the appropriate treatment for a patient who experienced a sudden seizure episode and became drowsy at the end of a dialysis session, with hypotension and pre-existing hyperkalemia and elevated urea levels?","o":[{"t":"Bumetanide"},{"t":"Ethacrynic acid"},{"t":"Nesiritide"},{"t":"IV Mannitol"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - IV Mannitol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome is a complication of hemodialysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It occurs due to rapid fluctuations of plasma osmolarity due to aggressive or frequent dialysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It causes cerebral edema leading to seizures which is managed by IV mannitol."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Bumetanide: Bumetanide is a loop diuretic that is used to treat edema in the body due to fluid retention."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ethacrynic acid: Ethacrynic acid is also a diuretic that functions similarly to loop-diuretics."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Nesiritide: Nesiritide is a natriuretic peptide that is used in patients with acutely decompensated congestive heart failure."}]},{"n":27,"q":"What is the most probable cause of the muteness, quadriplegia, and rigidity observed in an elderly female patient who had a previous medical history of severe vomiting and was aggressively treated for severe dehydration at a different hospital one week ago?","o":[{"t":"Malignant hyperthermia"},{"t":"Severe catatonia"},{"t":"Rapid sodium correction"},{"t":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Rapid sodium correction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rapid correction of sodium levels in chronic hyponatremia leads to Osmotic demyelination syndrome, earlier known as Central pontine myelinolysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized by demyelination in the descending tracts in the brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This manifests as quadriplegia, dysarthria, dysphagia, and altered mental status."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To avoid this chronic hyponatremia is corrected gradually- Do not exceed 8 mEq/L rise in serum Na+ in 24 hours."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Malignant hyperthermia: It is characterized by a rapid increase in body temperature, muscle rigidity, and other signs of a hypermetabolic state ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Severe catatonia: Catatonia is a neuropsychiatric syndrome characterized by a range of symptoms, including immobility, mutism, rigidity, and abnormal motor behaviors . However, catatonia is not typically associated with severe vomiting or dehydration."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS): It is characterized by hyperthermia, altered mental status, autonomic dysfunction, and generalized muscle rigidity."}]},{"n":28,"q":"A patient presented with complaints of pain in the flank region with hematuria. On investigation, X-ray shows multiple calcification (stones) in both kidneys. What is the probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Parathyroid Adenoma"},{"t":"Renal calculi"},{"t":"Polycystic kidney disease"},{"t":"CKD"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Renal calculi:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of flank pain, hematuria, and multiple calcifications seen on X-ray in both kidneys strongly suggests the presence of kidney stones."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features: Renal colic- spasmodic in nature and pain radiates towards the umblicus. Vomiting due to renogastric reflex. Gross hematuria as the edges of the stone cause damage to the ureter. Symptoms of urgency in females in case of lower ureteric stone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal colic- spasmodic in nature and pain radiates towards the umblicus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting due to renogastric reflex."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gross hematuria as the edges of the stone cause damage to the ureter."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of urgency in females in case of lower ureteric stone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Investigation of choice- Helical CT abdomen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal colic- spasmodic in nature and pain radiates towards the umblicus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting due to renogastric reflex."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gross hematuria as the edges of the stone cause damage to the ureter."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of urgency in females in case of lower ureteric stone."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A - Parathyroid Adenoma: Parathyroid adenoma refers to the enlargement of one or more of the parathyroid glands, which are responsible for regulating calcium levels in the body."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options C - Polycystic kidney disease: While PKD can lead to kidney-related symptoms, such as flank pain and hematuria, the presence of multiple calcifications (stones) in both kidneys is not a typical feature of this condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options D - CKD (Chronic Kidney Disease): While CKD can lead to various complications, such as the formation of kidney stones, the presence of multiple calcifications (stones) in both kidneys on X-ray is not specific to CKD."}]},{"n":29,"q":"What acid-base imbalance is most likely to be observed in a middle-aged man with chronic renal failure on dialysis who experiences sudden collapse in the emergency department and exhibits labored breathing, as evidenced by tall, tented T waves on the ECG?","o":[{"t":"pH-7.14, pCO 2 -20 mmHg, HCO 3 - 5 mEq/L"},{"t":"pH-7.14, pCO 2 -20 mmHg, HCO 3 - 34 mEq/L"},{"t":"pH-7.54, HCO 3 - 27 mEq/L, pCO 2 - 34 mmHg"},{"t":"pH-7.4, HCO 3 - 27 mEq/L, pCO 2 - 40 mmHg"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - pH-7.14, pCO 2 -20 mmHg, HCO 3 - 5 mEq/L:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low pH (acidotic), low pCO 2 and low HCO 3 indicates metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic acidosis is accompanied by hyperventilation (kussumaul breathing) leading to CO 2 washout (compensated)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In metabolic acidosis if PaCO 2 : Normal- Uncompensated Decreased- Partially compensated Increased- Mixed (Metabolic and respiratory acidosis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal- Uncompensated"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased- Partially compensated"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased- Mixed (Metabolic and respiratory acidosis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of tall, tented T waves on the ECG is a characteristic finding in severe hyperkalemia due to inverse relation of pH and K+."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the most likely acid-base imbalance in this patient is severe metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal- Uncompensated"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased- Partially compensated"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased- Mixed (Metabolic and respiratory acidosis)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - pH-7.14, pCO 2 -20 mmHg, HCO 3 - 34 mEq/L: In this option, the pH is low (acidic) at 7.14, indicating acidemia. The pCO 2 is low at 20 mmHg, suggesting respiratory alkalosis. However, the HCO 3 - level is elevated at 34 mEq/L, indicating metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - pH-7.54, HCO 3 - 27 mEq/L, pCO 2 - 34 mmHg: In this option, the pH is high (alkaline) at 7.54, indicating alkalemia. The pCO 2 is within the normal range at 34 mmHg, suggesting normal or compensated respiratory status. The HCO 3 - level is slightly elevated at 27 mEq/L, indicating metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - pH-7.4, HCO 3 - 27 mEq/L, pCO 2 - 40 mmHg: This combination of normal pH, normal pCO 2 , and normal HCO 3 - does not indicate any significant acid-base imbalance. Therefore, this option is less likely to be the correct acid-base imbalance in this case."}]},{"n":30,"q":"In a patient presenting with a seizure, when examining their urine osmolality (1000 mOsm/kg) and serum osmolality (270 mOsm/kg), what electrolyte abnormality can be anticipated?","o":[{"t":"Hypernatremia"},{"t":"Hyponatremia"},{"t":"Hyperkalaemia"},{"t":"Hypokalaemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Hyponatremia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyponatremia is the most common electrolyte imbalance encountered in hospitalised patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal plasma osmolality and increased urine osmolality indicates SIADH (Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In SIADH, due to increased ADH there is increased water absorption increasing the total body water leading to Euvolemic hyponatremia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The likely electrolyte imbalance is hyponatremia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Hypernatremia: Plasma osmolality in Hypernatremia would typically be associated with increased serum osmolality (>295 mOsm/kg) due to a deficit of water in relation to sodium."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hyperkalemia: Hyperkalemia refers to an elevated level of potassium in the blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypokalemia: Hypokalemia is a condition characterized by a low level of potassium in the blood."}]},{"n":31,"q":"A 40-year-old male presents with complains of episodes of palpitations, headache, and profuse sweating for the past few days. MIBG scan shows a deep seated tumour in the adrenal medulla. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the condition described?","o":[{"t":"Diagnosed by urine VMA & catecholamines"},{"t":"Surgical excision is the definitive treatment"},{"t":"Propranolol is given initially to manage hypertension"},{"t":"Can present as hypertension alone and sometimes with vomiting and pain abdomen"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Propranolol is given initially to manage hypertension:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above condition is characteristic of pheochromocytoma. Episosdes of palpitations, profuse sweating, and headache constitutes classic triad of pheochromocytoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of all three manifestations in association with hypertension makes pheochromocytoma a likely diagnosis. Hypertension is a dominant sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Propranolol is given initially to manage hypertension This statement is incorrect."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To control blood pressure alpha-adrenergic blockers like oral phenoxybenzamine is used."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Administering a beta-blocker without adequate alpha-blockade can worsen hypertension and lead to a hypertensive crisis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, it is essential to initiate alpha-blockade (e.g., with phenoxybenzamine) prior to beta-blockade in these patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Diagnosed by urine VMA & catecholamines : This statement is correct. Pheochromocytoma can be diagnosed by measuring levels of vanillylmandelic acid (VMA) and catecholamines (such as epinephrine and norepinephrine) in urine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Surgical excision is the definitive treatment: This statement is correct. Surgical excision is the definitive treatment for pheochromocytoma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Can present as hypertension alone and sometimes with vomiting and pain abdomen: This statement is correct. Pheochromocytoma can present with a variety of symptoms, but the most common clinical feature is hypertension. Other symptoms include episodic or paroxysmal hypertension, headache, palpitations, sweating, anxiety, flushing, vomiting, and abdominal pain."}]},{"n":32,"q":"Which of the options below is commonly linked to pauci-immune glomerulonephritis?","o":[{"t":"SLE nephritis"},{"t":"Anti-GBM glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"IgA nephropathy"},{"t":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA)"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pauci-immune glomerulonephritis refers to a type of kidney inflammation characterized by a lack of immune deposits within the glomeruli (the filtering units of the kidneys). It is typically associated with small-vessel vasculitides, which are a group of disorders involving inflammation of the blood vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA), formerly known as Wegener's granulomatosis, is one of the small-vessel vasculitides associated with pauci-immune glomerulonephritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features include fever, purulent rhinorrhea, nasal ulcers, sinus pain, polyarthralgias/arthritis, cough, hemoptysis, shortness of breath, microscopic hematuria."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - SLE nephritis: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that can affect multiple organs, including the kidneys. Lupus nephritis refers to kidney inflammation associated with SLE."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Anti-GBM glomerulonephritis: Anti-GBM (glomerular basement membrane) glomerulonephritis is a rare autoimmune disorder characterized by the presence of antibodies directed against the glomerular basement membrane."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - IgA nephropathy: IgA nephropathy, also known as Berger's disease, is a kidney disorder characterized by the accumulation of immunoglobulin A (IgA) within the glomeruli."}]},{"n":33,"q":"Which statement regarding syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"Urinary sodium <20 mEq/L"},{"t":"Serum sodium <135 mEq/L"},{"t":"Urine osmolality >100 mOsm/kg"},{"t":"Patient can be clinically euvolemic to hypervolemic"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Urinary sodium <20 mEq/L:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased ADH fl"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Extra stimulation of V2 receptor Causes more water reabsorption"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Total body water increases, while total body salt is normal"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Results in Euvolemic hyponatremia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is incorrect"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis of SIADH"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The urinary sodium concentration in SIADH is usually >40 mEq/L."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Serum sodium <135 mEq/L: SIADH is associated with hyponatremia, which is defined as serum sodium levels less than 135 mEq/L."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Urine osmolality >100 mOsm/kg: Urine osmolality is typically greater than 100 mOsm/kg, indicating the kidneys’ inability to appropriately dilute urine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Patient can be clinically euvolemic to hypervolemic: Patients with SIADH can present as clinically euvolemic (normal volume status) or hypervolemic (excess fluid volume)."}]},{"n":34,"q":"Which of the following condition is not associated with hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis?","o":[{"t":"Barter syndrome"},{"t":"Liddle syndrome"},{"t":"Gitelman syndrome"},{"t":"Gordon syndrome"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Gordon syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In Gordon syndrome, DCT is affected- Sodium chloride cotransporter."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gordon syndrome is also known as pseudohypoaldosteronism type II, it is a rare autosomal dominant disorder characterized by hypertension, hyperkalemia (high potassium levels), and metabolic acidosis. It is not associated with hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Gain of function causes an increase HTN, Hyperchloremi Hydrogen ions are retained due to in sodium and water c acidosis electroneutrality function of the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"kidney"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Barter syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Barter syndrome is rare autosomal recessive disorder that is associated with Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Liddle syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A genetic condition involving increased sodium reabsorption in the distal tubules, leading to hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Gitelman syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gitelman syndrome is a rare autosomal recessive disorder that is associated with Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis"}]},{"n":35,"q":"A 10 year old male child was brought with complains of headache, cola colored urine. His parents give history of throat pain 2 weeks ago. On examination, red blood cells casts are present in urine and his KFT is increased two fold. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Minimal change disease"},{"t":"Membranous Glomerulopathy"},{"t":"Post infectious Glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"IgA nephropathy"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Post infectious Glomerulonephritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The likely diagnosis of the above patient is Post-infectious Glomerulonephritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post-infectious Glomerulonephritis : This is a type of nephritic syndrome that occurs after an infection, most commonly caused by group A beta hemolytic streptococcus."},{"k":"img","v":6244},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Minimal change disease: Minimal change disease is characterized by minimal changes in the glomeruli on microscopic examination and is commonly seen in children. It typically presents with proteinuria, edema, and hypoalbuminemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Membranous Glomerulopathy: Membranous glomerulopathy characterized by thickening of the glomerular basement membrane due to immune complex deposits. It typically presents with proteinuria, edema, and hypoalbuminemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - IgA nephropathy: IgA nephropathy is the most common glomerulonephritis that develops in a patient with URTI within 48 hours."}]},{"n":36,"q":"A 30 year old man presents with sudden onset of a headache, palpitation, and profuse sweating. He had several such episodes in the past. During these episodes, his BP is found to be high. A 24 hour urinary metanephrine is elevated. What is the most likely etiology?","o":[{"t":"Fibromuscular dysplasia"},{"t":"Renal artery stenosis"},{"t":"Pheochromocytoma"},{"t":"Hyperthyroidism"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Pheochromocytoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical presentation and test results, the most likely etiology in this case is pheochromocytoma. The correct answer is: Pheochromocytoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pheochromocytoma, also known as chromaffinoma is a tumour arising from the chromaffin cells leading to excessive production and release of catecholamines."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"24 hours urinary fractioned metanephrine levels is checked for screening."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma fractioned metanephrine levels is the investigation of choice."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features of Pheochromocytoma Excess of catecholamines cause a1 stimulation causes vasoconstriction Excess activation of b1 and b2 Hypertension is episodic/Paroxysmal b1: Increased heart rate and palpitations Catecholamines have a short duration of action and b2: Fine tremors in fingers hence BP can be increased for only a few hours/minutes Diaphoresis: Sweaty palms/soles initially"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Can be triggered by position change, pregnancy, and urination Headache"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Great masquerader (Triad to suspect, not confirmatory) Mnemonic: PHD P alpitations H eadache/Hypertension D iaphoresis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Fibromuscular dysplasia: Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) is a non-inflammatory and non-atherosclerotic arterial disease that primarily affects the medium-sized arteries."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Renal artery stenosis: Renal artery stenosis can cause hypertension, but it does not typically present with sudden-onset headache, palpitations, and profuse sweating."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hyperthyroidism: Hyperthyroidism can cause symptoms such as palpitations and sweating, sudden-onset headache and episodes of high blood pressure are not typical features of this condition."}]},{"n":37,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents to the OPD with palpitation and weight loss. On examination of her legs the following lesion was found which on applying pressure, the indentation does not persist on release. ECG shows sinus rhtyhm. What is the probable cause of the edema?","o":[{"t":"Myxedema"},{"t":"Nephrotic syndrome"},{"t":"Chronic lymphangitis"},{"t":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Nephrotic syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical presentation and examination findings, the most probable cause for the edema, in this case, is Nephrotic syndrome. The correct answer is: Nephrotic syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nephrotic syndrome classically presents with: Heavy proteinuria Minimal hematuria Hypoalbuminemia Hypercholesterolemia Edema Hypertension"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heavy proteinuria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Minimal hematuria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypoalbuminemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypercholesterolemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Edema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypertension"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heavy proteinuria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Minimal hematuria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypoalbuminemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypercholesterolemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Edema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypertension"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Myxedema: Myxedema refers to severe hypothyroidism and is not consistent with the presentation of palpitation and weight loss."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Chronic lymphangitis: Chronic lymphangitis refers to inflammation of the lymphatic vessels and can cause lymphedema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Dilated cardiomyopathy: Dilated cardiomyopathy presents with pitting edema."}],"qi":[4614]},{"n":38,"q":"A hepatitis B positive person came to your OPD on routine urine massive proteinuria (> 3.5 g/day), Further renal biopsy was performed identify the disease?","o":[{"t":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"},{"t":"Membranous glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"Minimal change disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Membranous glomerulonephritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the presentation of a patient with Hepatitis B and heavy proteinuria the most likely finding on renal biopsy would be membranous glomerulonephritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Membranous glomerulonephritis is a type of glomerular disease characterized by the thickening and immune complex deposition along the glomerular basement membrane. It can be associated with various underlying conditions, including solid tumors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diffuse capillary and GBM thickening is characteristic finding"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS): FSGS is characterized by scarring and sclerosis of certain glomeruli in a focal and segmental pattern. this option is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN): MPGN is characterized by both thickening and proliferation of the glomerular basement membrane. This option is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Minimal change disease: Minimal change disease is a common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children but is less common in adults. This option is incorrect."}],"qi":[6199]},{"n":39,"q":"A patient is having loose stools around 20 per day and his urine frequency is reduced now. Which of the following laboratory values is seen in patient?","o":[{"t":"BUN: Cr >40:1, urine Na <40, FENa <1% urine osmolality >500"},{"t":"BUN: Cr >20:1, urine Na <20, FENa <1% urine osmolality >500"},{"t":"BUN: Cr >20:1, urine Na <20, FENa <1% urine osmolality >250"},{"t":"BUN: Cr >40:1, urine Na <20, FENa <1% urine osmolality >250"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - BUN: Cr >20:1, urine Na <20, FENa <1% urine osmolality >500:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct answer is: BUN: Cr >20:1, urine Na <20, FENa <1% urine osmolality >500."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BUN: Cr >20:1, urine Na <20, FENa <1% urine osmolality >500: This option matches the typical laboratory values seen in the described patient. The BUN: Cr ratio is increased, urine sodium is low, FENa is less than 1%, and urine osmolality is greater than 500."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These findings suggest prerenal azotemia, a condition characterized by decreased renal perfusion leading to reduced urine output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes of pre-renal azotemia Hypovolemia Decreased CO in cardiomyopathy- cardiorenal syndrome Decreased effective circulating fluid volume Drugs like NSAIDS, ACEi"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypovolemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased CO in cardiomyopathy- cardiorenal syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased effective circulating fluid volume"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs like NSAIDS, ACEi"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypovolemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased CO in cardiomyopathy- cardiorenal syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased effective circulating fluid volume"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs like NSAIDS, ACEi"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":40,"q":"An 8 year old girl presents with abrupt onset of fever, oliguria, nausea, smoky urine, and puffy eyes. The parents say that she had lesions on her face similar to the ones shown in the image below, 2 weeks ago which are now resolving. Which of the following findings will be present in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Nephrotic range proteinuria"},{"t":"RBC’s in urine"},{"t":"Derangement of liver enzymes"},{"t":"Neurological symptoms"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - RBC’s in urine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the information provided, the most likely finding in this 8-year-old girl would be RBC's in the urine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The characteristic skin lesions described by the parents two weeks ago, which are now resolving, are consistent with a previous streptococcal infection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type 3 hypersentivity reaction caused by group A beta hemolytic streptococci."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The urine microscopy shows > 5 RBC/HPF or gross hematuria (glomerular bleeding)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other investigations KFT- increased two times. Increased potassium Demonstration of antibodies: Anti-DNAse, Anti-Hyaluronidase, ASO titer Immunofluorescence study- Starry Sky appearance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"KFT- increased two times."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased potassium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Demonstration of antibodies: Anti-DNAse, Anti-Hyaluronidase, ASO titer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunofluorescence study- Starry Sky appearance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"KFT- increased two times."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased potassium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Demonstration of antibodies: Anti-DNAse, Anti-Hyaluronidase, ASO titer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunofluorescence study- Starry Sky appearance"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Nephrotic range proteinuria: Nephrotic range proteinuria, characterized by heavy proteinuria (>3.5 grams per day), is not a typical feature of PSGN. PSGN is more commonly associated with hematuria and mild to moderate proteinuria."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C & D - Derangement of liver enzymes and Neurological symptoms: Derangement of liver enzymes and neurological symptoms are not typically associated with PSGN. PSGN primarily affects the kidneys and may present with symptoms related to kidney dysfunction, such as oliguria and smoky urine."}],"qi":[6206]},{"n":41,"q":"Hemodialysis is an indication for all the below conditions except?","o":[{"t":"Uremic encephalopathy"},{"t":"Peripheral neuropathy"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Uremic pericarditis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Peripheral Neuropathy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Not an Immediate Indication for Hemodialysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral neuropathy in CKD patients develops gradually and is less likely to improve rapidly with hemodialysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It often requires long-term management of underlying renal disease and symptomatic treatment rather than acute dialysis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Uremic encephalopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indication for Hemodialysis : Uremic encephalopathy is a severe complication of chronic kidney disease (CKD) where the accumulation of uremic toxins affects brain function. Hemodialysis is indicated to rapidly remove these toxins from the blood and alleviate symptoms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Metabolic acidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indication for Hemodialysis : Severe metabolic acidosis, particularly when refractory to medical management, is an indication for hemodialysis. Dialysis can help correct the acid-base imbalance by removing excess acids from the blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Uremic pericarditis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indication for Hemodialysis : Uremic pericarditis is an inflammation of the pericardium due to the accumulation of uremic toxins in the blood. Hemodialysis is indicated to remove these toxins and reduce the inflammation."}]},{"n":42,"q":"Which of the following options represents the most severe manifestation of Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)?","o":[{"t":"Decreased GFR"},{"t":"Increased creatinine"},{"t":"Hyperkalaemia"},{"t":"Proteinuria"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Hyperkalaemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperkalemia refers to an elevated level of potassium in the blood. In the context of chronic kidney disease (CKD), it is considered one of the most severe manifestations. As kidney function declines, the ability of the kidneys to excrete potassium decreases, leading to its accumulation in the bloodstream."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Electrolyte imbalances in CKD Hyperkalemia- refractory to medical therapy is an indication for CKD Hypokalemia Hyperphosphatemia Hypocalcemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperkalemia- refractory to medical therapy is an indication for CKD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperphosphatemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypocalcemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperkalemia- refractory to medical therapy is an indication for CKD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperphosphatemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypocalcemia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Decreased GFR: Decreased glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is a hallmark of CKD and represents impaired kidney function. While decreased GFR is a key diagnostic criterion for CKD, it alone does not indicate the severity of the disease. The progression of CKD and its complications, such as hyperkalemia, are influenced by factors beyond the GFR."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Increased creatinine: Elevated creatinine levels are indicative of reduced kidney function in CKD, they do not directly reflect the severity of the disease. Creatinine levels can vary depending on various factors, including muscle mass, age, and hydration status."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Proteinuria: Proteinuria, the presence of excess protein in the urine, is a common finding in CKD. While it is an important marker of kidney damage and can be associated with disease progression, it is not considered the most severe manifestation of CKD."}]},{"n":43,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a patient who has symptoms of frequent urination, nocturia, and enuresis, along with a 24-hour urine volume of 7 liters, a urine osmolarity of 260 mOsm/L, an ADH assay result of 0.8 pg/ml, and a brain MRI showing no bright spot on T1 weighted images?","o":[{"t":"Nephrogenic DI"},{"t":"Primary polydipsia"},{"t":"Pituitary DI"},{"t":"Mannitol infusion"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Pituitary DI (diabetes insipidus)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation of frequent urination, nocturia, and enuresis along with a 24-hour urine volume of 7 liters and low urine osmolarity indicates the inability to concentrate urine, which is a characteristic feature of diabetes insipidus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The low ADH (antidiuretic hormone) level detected by the assay suggests and absence of the bright spot on T1 weighted MRI is consistent with a lack of posterior pituitary gland hyperintensity, further supporting the diagnosis of pituitary DI."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In pituitary DI, the polyuria results in a small (1–2%) decrease in body water and a commensurate increase in plasma osmolarity and sodium that stimulates thirst and a compensatory increase in water intake."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As a result, hypernatremia and other overt physical or laboratory signs of dehydration do not develop unless the patient also has a defect in thirst or fails to increase fluid intake for some other reason."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Nephrogenic DI: This statement is incorrect. Nephrogenic DI is characterized by impaired renal response to ADH, resulting in the inability to concentrate urine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Primary polydipsia: This statement is incorrect. Primary polydipsia refers to excessive water intake leading to water diuresis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Mannitol infusion: This statement is incorrect. Mannitol infusion, a diuretic agent, is used to increase urine output in specific clinical situations."}]},{"n":44,"q":"AKIN and RIFLE criteria are used to classify which of the following conditions?","o":[{"t":"Acute kidney injury"},{"t":"Chronic renal failure"},{"t":"Acute glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"Nephrotic syndrome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Acute kidney injury:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AKIN (Acute Kidney Injury Network) and RIFLE (Risk, Injury, Failure, Loss of kidney function, and End-stage kidney disease) criteria are used to classify and diagnose acute kidney injury (AKI). These criteria provide a standardized approach for assessing the severity of AKI based on changes in serum creatinine levels, urine output, and the duration of kidney dysfunction."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteri AKIN RIFLE a"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definiti Based on changes in serum Based on changes in serum on creatinine and urine output. creatinine, urine output, and GFR."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serum Increase in serum creatinine ‡0.3 R (Risk): Increase in serum Creati mg/dL within 48 hours or ‡1.5 times creatinine ‡1.5 times baseline or nine baseline within 7 days. GFR decrease >25%."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"I (Injury): Increase in serum creatinine ‡2 times baseline or GFR decrease >50%. F (Failure): Increase in serum creatinine ‡3 times baseline or GFR decrease >75%."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Urine Decreased urine output (<0.5 O (Loss): Persistent acute kidney Output mL/kg/hour for >6 hours). injury with complete loss of kidney"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"function for >4 weeks. E (End-stage kidney disease): Persistent acute kidney injury with complete loss of kidney function for >3 months."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stagin AKIN stage 1: Increase in serum RIFLE classifies severity based on g creatinine ‡0.3 mg/dL or ‡1.5 times the R, I, F, O, and E criteria."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"baseline or urine output <0.5 mL/kg/hour for 6-12 hours. AKIN stage 2: Increase in serum creatinine ‡2 times baseline or urine output <0.5 mL/kg/hour for ‡12 hours. AKIN stage 3: Increase in serum creatinine ‡3 times baseline or serum creatinine ‡4.0 mg/dL or initiation of renal replacement therapy (RRT) or urine output <0.3 mL/kg/hour for ‡24 hours or anuria for ‡12 hours."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinica Used in research, clinical trials, and Widely used in research, clinical l Appli clinical practice for AKI diagnosis trials, and clinical practice for AKI cation and staging. diagnosis, classification, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"outcome assessment."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteri AKIN RIFLE a"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Limitat Relies on serum creatinine and Similar limitations as AKIN criteria. ions urine output, which can be More recent studies suggest that"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"influenced by factors such as age, RIFLE may be more predictive of muscle mass, fluid status, and mortality and need for RRT. medications."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Chronic renal failure: AKIN and RIFLE criteria are specifically designed to classify acute kidney injury and are not applicable to chronic renal failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acute glomerulonephritis: AKIN and RIFLE criteria are not used to classify acute glomerulonephritis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Nephrotic syndrome: AKIN and RIFLE criteria are not used to classify nephrotic syndrome."}]},{"n":45,"q":"A woman presents to the OPD with fever, arthralgia, ulcers, fatigue for the past six months, and new-onset hematuria. Urine examination reveals RBC casts and proteinuria. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Acute interstitial nephritis"},{"t":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"},{"t":"Lupus nephritis"},{"t":"IgA nephropathy"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Lupus nephritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical features of fever, arthralgia, ulcers, fatigue, and hematuria in addition to RBC casts and proteinuria on urine tests imply a disease of lupus nephritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lupus nephritis is a secondary nephrotic syndrome, a common complication of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and can present accompanying differing dispassionate exhibitions, containing hematuria, proteinuria, and renal degradation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of RBC casts and proteinuria is suggestive of glomerular involvement, which is a universal feature of lupus nephritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In addition, the patient's syndromes of frenzy, arthralgia, and ulcers are still average features of SLE."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Acute interstitial nephritis: It is characterized by inflammation and damage to the renal interstitium and tubules, and can present with syndromes such as fever, rash, and eosinophilia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis : It is a type 3 hypersensitivity reaction after an infection accompanying group A beta-hemolytic streptococcus . It usually presents with cola coloured, smoky urine, headache and renal insufficiency leading to features of uremia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - IgA nephropathy: IgA nephropathy/Berger's disease is a the most common cause of glomerulonephritisis. It presents with hematuria along with pharyngitis."}]}]);
